    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 291807\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1107 AM MST Sat Aug 29 2026\n\n.UPDATE...18Z Aviation Discussion\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Extreme Heat Warnings go through early this evening before a\n  noticeable cool down begins Sunday.\n\n- A Flood Watch has been issued for south-central Arizona from\n  late this afternoon through Saturday night. Localized rainfall\n  amounts of up to around two inches will be possible.\n\n- Showers and thunderstorms, some potentially strong to severe, \n  are expected starting late afternoon into tonight. Additional \n  rounds of showers and storms are also possible Sunday and \n  Monday.\n\n- Near to below normal temperatures are forecast starting Sunday\n  before warming again during the latter part of next week.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nDespite the extreme heat still being present through this\nafternoon, it will quickly come to an end by Sunday as everything\nis pointing toward an active period of weather starting late this\nafternoon. The subtropical ridge is currently in a weakening \nphase while also slowly shifting eastward through New Mexico. The \nplacement of the ridge is allowing for modest moisture advection \ninto Arizona with 1\"+ PWATs now covering all but far \neastern/northeastern parts of the state to as high as 1.6\" across \nthe southwest portion. Low level moisture has so far been slow to \nincrease, but we are expecting low level mixing ratios to increase\nto around 10 g/kg by late this afternoon and 11-12 g/kg by early \nSunday. \n\nIn addition to the increase in moisture, the upper levels will\nbecome much more conducive for storm development by later today \nas a Pacific trough noses into our region from the northwest. \nForecast soundings for south-central Arizona by late this \nafternoon show a mostly uncapped environment with MUCAPEs between \n1000-1500 J/kg and very high DCAPEs of 1500-1800 J/kg. The only \ningredient lacking for severe weather for today will be good \nshear. However, even without good wind shear we are expecting to \nhave enough instability, high DCAPEs, and upper level support for \nsome strong to possibility severe storms to develop. HREF guidance\nhints at a couple different areas supporting a strong to severe \nwind threat today, one over the Imperial Valley and the other \ncentered over Pinal into Maricopa Counties. CAMs favor storm \ndevelopment first across Pima County late this afternoon before \nshifting northward with further development expected into south- \ncentral Arizona. The strong to severe wind threat should mainly \nfall between 5-10 pm this evening before transitioning to mostly a\nheavy rainfall/flood only threat from late evening into the \novernight hours. \n\nThe amount of instability and upper level support available is\nlikely to allow for a few rounds of showers and thunderstorms\nlater today and tonight and this is where the heavy rainfall and\nflood threat stems from. Moisture levels are not expected to be\nall that much above normal, but they will be plenty adequate to\nsupport rainfall rates of 1.5-2.5\"/hour with the strongest storms.\nTraining of storms may also be an issue, especially over higher \nterrain areas. The heavy rainfall/flood threat is high enough for \nus to issue a Flood Watch from late afternoon through 5 AM Sunday \nmorning across Maricopa, Pinal, and Gila Counties with the peak of\nthe event likely occurring during the evening hours.\n\nSunday is still looking quite good for storm potential with\nmoisture improving slightly over today, while the peak of the\nupper level support is expected to be in place over Arizona.\nSunday morning and even a good portion of the afternoon may be a\nrecovery period after the activity from today/tonight, but models\nshow enough sunshine for instability to become surface based again\nby late afternoon with MUCAPEs again reaching 1000-1500 J/kg. The\nstrong to severe wind threat for Sunday should again be present,\nbut likely not to the degree of what is expected today. Assuming\nthe atmosphere can recover enough, chances are we will face\nanother round of localized heavy rainfall and flood potential from\nlate Sunday afternoon through Sunday evening. This is reflected by\nthe slight risk on WPC's Day 2 ERO.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nModel uncertainty remains quite high for much of next week,\nespecially on Monday as at least the eastern half of Arizona\nshould retain good moisture, while an upper level low swings\nthrough Nevada during the first part of the day. We are still\nunsure how much instability will be present and if the low will\nmove to the north too quickly, but guidance is still favoring\n30-60% PoPs from Phoenix and areas to the east of Phoenix for \nmuch of Monday. Monday is likely to be very conditional on any\nstrong to severe storm threat due to the question of instability,\nbut we are expecting better wind shear on Monday. The passing\ntrough to our northwest should also begin to sweep drier air\nthrough a good portion of our area on Monday, but this may only\nimpact western areas during the first part of the day leaving \nenough moisture from Maricopa County eastward. This drying and \nthe eventual loss of upper level support is likely to mostly end \nrain chances on Tuesday before returning by next Wednesday or \nThursday. Guidance suggests the tropics across the eastern Pacific\nwill remain very active next week likely leading to more moisture\ngetting advected into the Desert Southwest during the latter half\nof next week. Forecast temperatures are shown returning into or \neven a few degrees below the normal range early next week before \nrising midweek as conditions temporarily dry out. The subtropical \nridge is also expected to nudge back into our region from the east\nhelping to boost temperatures back to or a few degrees above \nnormal at some point late next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1807Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\n\nThe main aviation concern will be thunderstorms, gusty\noutflows,and blowing dust this evening and into the\novernight/early morning period. Model ensembles continue to point\ntowards an active night with two potential outflow events. The \nfirst, a northerly flow from thunderstorm development to the \nnorth of the terminal area. Then, a southerly to southeasterly \ngusty outflow with gusts between 25-30kts possible from \nthunderstorm development to south. Look for the northerly event to\noccur around the 00-02Z timeframe while the southerly outflow \nduring the 02-05Z timeframe. TEMPO lines are now in the TAFs for \nall terminals and may need to be adjusted as the event progresses.\nAny outflows from the S/SE would also bring BLDU/HZ with them, \nmostly expected to impact KPHX and KIWA, potentially reaching the \nnorthern terminals. Winds are currently forecasted to settle back \nout of the east for the overnight period, once storms subside, but\nthey could remain highly VRB until morning. Mostly SCT mid/high \ncloud decks will be common through the period, with the lowest \nCIGs forecasted at 10kft during thunderstorm activity. Potential \nthunderstorm activity for Sunday is forecast, but highly dependent\non how storms forecast for this evening into tomorrow morning \nunfold.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nThe main aviation concern is a low probability(<30%)chance for \nthunderstorms this evening at both terminals. Before then winds \nwill tend to follow their typical diurnal patterns. Shifting \nbetween southerly and southwesterly at KBLH and southeasterly to \nwesterly at KIPL, favoring a westerly component for a majority of \nthe period. Wind speeds will generally be aob 10 kt, with some \ngusts up around 20kt expected this afternoon at both terminals. A \nPROB30 is reflected in the TAFs for KIPL for TS from 23-01Z \ntonight and 00-02Z at KBLH, but the main concern will be the \nincrease in gust speeds during that timeframe. Expect gusts \nbetween 25-30kts. Otherwise, FEW-SCT mid/high cloud decks will be \ncommon throughout the period with periods of mostly clear skies.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nA pattern shift this weekend will lead to increasing moisture and\nvery good chances for showers and thunderstorms across the eastern\ndistricts. Gusty winds, heavy rainfall, and localized flooding\nwill all be possible across south-central Arizona beginning late\nthis afternoon. The western districts will generally stay dry.\nExpect MinRHs of 15-20% today areawide before rising to 25-35% \nacross the eastern districts starting Sunday. Temperatures are \nalso forecast to drop to near normal readings starting Sunday.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST this evening for AZZ530>555-\n     559-560.\n\n     Blowing Dust Advisory from 4 PM this afternoon to 9 PM MST this \n     evening for AZZ539-550-551-553-554-559.\n\n     Flood Watch from 5 PM MST this afternoon through late tonight \n     for AZZ534-537-538-540>563.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT this evening for CAZ562-\n     565>567-569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...95/Ryan\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
